/** @page fuzzing_corpus Corpus and compatibility keys

A campaign's merged **corpus** is its most valuable output: a compact set of
inputs that together reach a lot of code. Promoting it into the app lets future
local campaigns start from that coverage instead of rediscovering it every time.
CI keeps its own corpus and does not depend on it (@ref cor_ci).

@section cor_promote Promoting a corpus

After a good campaign, promote its merged corpus with `corpus.py promote`:

@verbatim
"$BOLOS_SDK"/fuzzing/scripts/corpus.py promote \
  <app>/.fuzz-artifacts/<run>/targets/<target>/corpus \
  <app>/fuzzing/base-corpus.zip
@endverbatim

This writes two tracked files next to each other:

- `base-corpus.zip` — the inputs, packed with the Python standard library (no
  `zip`/`unzip` tool required),
- `base-corpus.compat-key` — the sidecar key that ties the archive to a build.

The `.compat-key` is metadata, not a fuzz input, so it is stored **beside** the
archive, never inside it.

@section cor_reuse Reusing a corpus

Reuse is automatic. On the next run the campaign stages `base-corpus.zip` into
the starting corpus — but only when its sidecar key matches the current build. A mismatch is reported and the stale corpus is
skipped, so a changed binary is never fed inputs shaped for an old one. Set
`BASE_CORPUS_ZIP=` (empty) to skip a promoted corpus for one run.

The end-to-end loop:

1. Run a campaign (@ref fuzzing_running).
2. `corpus.py promote` its corpus to `fuzzing/base-corpus.zip`.
3. Commit `base-corpus.zip` and `base-corpus.compat-key`.
4. The next local campaign starts from it automatically.

@section cor_ci Corpus in CI

CI does not rely on the promoted corpus. ClusterFuzzLite publishes each target's
corpus as a `cifuzz-corpus-<target>` artifact and every run starts from the newest
one, so the CI corpus grows and is pruned from run to run without any commit (see
@ref fuzzing_ci).

The promoted corpus is also normally skipped in CI: the key depends on the
invariant model, which depends on the toolchain, and CI builds with the OSS-Fuzz
Clang. The "Build Fuzzers" step then reports the base corpus as incompatible and
logs both keys (`compat_key: <key>` for each target). That message is expected
and does not affect the CI corpus.

@section cor_key The compatibility key

The key is:

@verbatim
sha256( prefix_size || sha256(fuzz_globals.zon) || fuzzer || harness_version )
@endverbatim

So it changes when the prefix size changes, when the invariant model changes
(a new sanitizer, an SDK/toolchain change that shifts globals), or when you bump
`harness_version` in the @ref fuzzing_manifest "manifest". When the key changes,
the old base corpus no longer matches: rerun a campaign and promote again to
refresh it.

@warning A refusal is easy to miss. `run_single_target` is invoked as
`if ! run_single_target ...`, which disables `errexit` for its whole body, so the
campaign continues from generated seeds alone and still reports a coverage number.
Check for the warning before trusting it: bootstrapping from a few dozen seeds instead
of a few thousand evolved inputs changes the result materially.

@section cor_when When to promote

- After a long run that reached coverage you want to keep.
- After a change that invalidated the key (the previous corpus is now skipped),
  so local campaigns do not restart from generated seeds.

One promoted corpus per target is the intended shape.

*/
